# External Data and Integrations

## Source policy
- Prefer official APIs and original community-maintained sources when they provide the required data.
- Trusted community sources are allowed when their provenance, maintenance status, and usage conditions can be established.
- Attribute external datasets and document their source, retrieval time, license/terms where applicable, and any transformations.
- Do not scrape or reuse data in ways that violate source terms, access controls, or reasonable rate limits.
- Never invent API endpoints, response schemas, authentication methods, CORS behavior, or service capabilities. Verify them from authoritative documentation or controlled tests.

## Hourly freshness target
- Treat hourly refresh as the desired freshness target, not a guarantee of successful hourly updates.
- Apply refresh cadence by dataset and source constraints. Server/player status may warrant frequent checks; relatively static map metadata may only need a change check.
- Schedule GitHub Actions away from the top of the hour (for example, at minute 17) to reduce contention with common scheduled-job peaks.
- Account for scheduled-workflow delays, skipped runs, API outages, rate limits, and source maintenance.
- Do not report a dataset as current merely because a scheduled workflow ran; record the last successful source retrieval and validation time.

## Hybrid acquisition and fallback
- Evaluate direct browser API access where CORS, authentication, reliability, and rate limits permit.
- Use scheduled GitHub Actions to retrieve, validate, normalize, and publish static JSON snapshots when direct access is unsuitable or when a stable fallback is needed.
- The frontend should use available live data where reliable and fall back to the latest validated snapshot or cached last-known-good data.
- Keep the last-known-good dataset if a refresh fails. Never replace valid data with an empty, partial, or invalid response without an explicit policy.
- Design the frontend so the static GitHub Pages site remains usable when external services are unavailable.
- Do not assume GitHub Pages can run server-side code. Keep secrets in GitHub Actions secrets or another approved server-side secret store; never expose them in client-side code or public artifacts.

## Data quality and provenance
- Validate HTTP status, content type, schema, required fields, identifiers, timestamps, and reasonable value ranges before publishing data.
- Detect duplicate identifiers and unexpected record-count changes.
- Preserve source timestamps separately from retrieval and publication timestamps.
- Record source name, source URL, retrieval time, validation result, and dataset version where practical.
- Clearly distinguish live, cached, snapshot, stale, estimated, and inferred values in the UI.
- Do not present estimated regional latency as measured ping. Only label ping as measured when it comes from an actual measurement.
- Avoid mixing datasets with different definitions or time windows without explaining the difference.
- Never fabricate records, rankings, player counts, server availability, map ratings, or update times.

## Reliability and operations
- Use bounded timeouts, sensible retry/backoff, and concurrency limits.
- Respect API rate limits and caching headers where provided.
- Make ingestion idempotent so repeated runs do not create duplicate or corrupt data.
- Keep workflow permissions minimal. Pin third-party GitHub Actions to reviewed versions or immutable references where practical.
- Ensure a failed source does not prevent unrelated datasets from refreshing when they can be processed independently.
- Log enough information to diagnose failures without leaking credentials.
- Document manual recovery and how to inspect the last successful update.

## Investigation required before implementation
- Verify each candidate API/source, its current endpoints, authentication requirements, CORS policy, rate limits, terms, and data fields.
- Determine which datasets can genuinely refresh hourly and which require a slower cadence.
- Test GitHub Actions access and publication permissions before relying on the workflow.
- Record unresolved limitations rather than silently assuming the hybrid strategy is feasible for every dataset.
